"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][10]={"university_physics_en:M10-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M10-L1 – Electrostatic Boundary-Value Problems<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor: Advanced Electrodynamics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Gauss’s law, vector calculus and\nboundary conditions; M8-L8. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M9&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive Poisson’s and Laplace’s\nequations, solve a planar boundary-value problem and state\nuniqueness.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Potential as a Field Problem<\/h2>\n<p>An electric field is not set merely by the charge at a chosen point:\nconductors, geometry and surroundings shape the potential throughout\nspace. In static equilibrium the field is curl-free and may be written\nas the gradient of a scalar potential,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol E=-\\nabla\\phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoundary-value problems specify a charge density together with either\npotential on the boundary (Dirichlet data) or its normal derivative\n(Neumann data). Where no charge is present the governing equation\nbecomes Laplace’s equation. Its solutions are harmonic functions, which\nhave no strict interior extrema unless constant on a connected domain.\nNumerical field solvers approximate the same mathematical problem on a\nmesh.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From Maxwell to Poisson’s Equation<\/h2>\n<p>Gauss’s law,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol E=\\rho/\\varepsilon_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncombined with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol E=-\\nabla\\phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>∇<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mi>ρ<\/mi><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla^2\\phi=-\\frac{\\rho}{\\varepsilon_0}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This is Poisson’s equation in vacuum. In a charge-free region\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nit reduces to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>∇<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla^2\\phi=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn Cartesian coordinates the Laplacian is the sum of second spatial\nderivatives,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>∇<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla^2=\\partial_x^2+\\partial_y^2+\\partial_z^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA conductor’s surface is equipotential; in an ideal conductor at\nelectrostatic equilibrium the internal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvanishes. Drawing field lines does not itself specify boundary data:\nprescribed potential values or fluxes are required for a definite\nmathematical solution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Parallel Electrodes<\/h2>\n<p>Two ideal extended plates at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare held at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi(0)=V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi(d)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAssume no intervening charge\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nand neglect fringing. Translation symmetry reduces Laplace’s equation to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm d^2\\phi/\\mathrm dx^2=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhose general solution is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi(x)=A+Bx<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe boundary values give<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>V<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mi>x<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>V<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi(x)=V\\left(1-\\frac{x}{d}\\right),\\qquad E_x=\\frac{V}{d}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=12\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.004<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=0.004\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_x=3000\\,\\mathrm{V/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe illustration displays planar equipotentials and a uniform field from\nhigher to lower potential. It ceases to be exact near the plate\nedges.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_144_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two plates with equipotential lines and aligned electric-field arrows\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two plates with equipotential lines and\naligned electric-field arrows<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Ideal parallel-plate approximation without fringe\nfields.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">State Poisson’s equation in vacuum.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=9\\,\\mathrm V<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=3\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϕ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndetermined only up to a constant for pure Neumann data?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>∇<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla^2\\phi=-\\rho/\\varepsilon_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.003<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_x=9/0.003=3000\\,\\mathrm{V/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Derivatives do not change under\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>↦<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi\\mapsto\\phi+C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nflux data alone cannot fix the constant.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Boundary Conditions and Uniqueness<\/h2>\n<p>If two solutions have identical charge density and Dirichlet boundary\nvalues, their difference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>u<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis harmonic and vanishes on the boundary. Integrating\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><msup><mi>∇<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u\\nabla^2u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nby parts over the volume gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∫<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\int_\\Omega|\\nabla u|^2\\,\\mathrm dV=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\ntherefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthroughout the connected region. This illustrates the uniqueness theorem\nfor regular Dirichlet problems. With purely Neumann conditions, an\nadditive constant remains undetermined and the total prescribed flux\nmust agree with the integrated charge. At interfaces, derivative\nconditions depend on material permittivity. A schematic drawing cannot\nreplace a check of these hypotheses.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Boundary conditions define the static problem. Time-dependent fields\nadditionally require a vector potential and a gauge choice.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Time-Dependent Potentials and\nGauge Freedom<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/electromagnetism/\">David Tong – Electromagnetism<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Individual Chapters 1–6, lines 4, 14–17; Subject relevance to lesson: M10-L1 – Electrostatic Boundary-Value Problems · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-303-linear-partial-differential-equations-fall-2006/pages/syllabus/\">18.303 Linear Partial Differential Equations – Syllabus (2006)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Outline I–VI; Subject relevance to lesson: M10-L1 – Electrostatic Boundary-Value Problems · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M10-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M10-L2 – Time-Dependent Potentials and Gauge Freedom<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor: Advanced Electrodynamics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Maxwell equations, divergence, curl\nand wave equation. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Relate scalar and vector\npotentials and distinguish Lorenz from Coulomb gauge.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why One Scalar Is No Longer Enough<\/h2>\n<p>When magnetic fields change with time, Faraday’s law gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla\\times\\boldsymbol E=-\\partial_t\\boldsymbol B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngenerally nonzero. The electric field\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan therefore no longer be reconstructed from a purely static scalar\npotential. Since\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol B=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none may locally write\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol B=\\nabla\\times\\boldsymbol A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTogether with a scalar potential\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϕ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis produces a linked pair of field definitions. Potentials are useful\nmathematical variables, but not merely typography: their global\nstructure can affect observable phases in quantum mechanics. The\ntreatment here is classical and begins within a simply connected\nregion.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Potentials and Gauge Freedom<\/h2>\n<p>The complete relations are<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol B=\\nabla\\times\\boldsymbol A,\\qquad\\boldsymbol E=-\\nabla\\phi-\\partial_t\\boldsymbol A.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For any sufficiently smooth function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\chi(\\boldsymbol r,t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe replacements\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>χ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol A&#39;=\\boldsymbol A+\\nabla\\chi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>χ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi&#39;=\\phi-\\partial_t\\chi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nleave the fields invariant:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>χ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla\\times\\nabla\\chi=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the extra mixed derivatives in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncancel. The Lorenz condition is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol A+c^{-2}\\partial_t\\phi=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn vacuum with sources, it gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>□<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>ρ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo>□<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑱<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Box\\phi=-\\rho/\\varepsilon_0,\\qquad\\Box\\boldsymbol A=-\\mu_0\\boldsymbol J,<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>where\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>□<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>∇<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msubsup><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Box=\\nabla^2-c^{-2}\\partial_t^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCoulomb gauge instead imposes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol A=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\noffering different computational properties.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Two Potential Pairs for the Same Fields<\/h2>\n<p>Start with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟎<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol A=\\boldsymbol 0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso both fields vanish. A gauge function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\chi(x,t)=axt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfor constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith the appropriate units, gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒆<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol A&#39;=at\\,\\boldsymbol e_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi&#39;=-ax<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTherefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∇<\/mi><msup><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒆<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\nabla\\phi&#39;=a\\boldsymbol e_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhereas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒆<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\partial_t\\boldsymbol A&#39;=-a\\boldsymbol e_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nin total\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol E&#39;=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLikewise\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol B&#39;=\\nabla\\times\\boldsymbol A&#39;=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTwo visibly different sets of potentials describe exactly the same\nclassical fields. The diagram contrasts potential representations while\nretaining an identical physical field.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_110_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two potential representations each producing zero electric and magnetic fields\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two potential representations each\nproducing zero electric and magnetic fields<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Different potentials can represent the same E and B\nfields.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">State the gauge transformation of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϕ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>χ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\chi(x,t)=axt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ntest whether transformed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nvanishes.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What condition defines Lorenz gauge?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>χ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol A&#39;=\\boldsymbol A+\\nabla\\chi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>χ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi&#39;=\\phi-\\partial_t\\chi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Yes:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∇<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒆<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\nabla(-ax)-\\partial_t(at\\boldsymbol e_x)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol A+c^{-2}\\partial_t\\phi=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Gauges, Causality and Limitations<\/h2>\n<p>In Lorenz gauge the potentials obey wave equations, for which\nretarded solutions connect measured fields to sources at earlier times.\nAn apparently instantaneous Coulomb scalar potential does not transmit a\nsignal faster than light: the vector-potential contribution must be\nincluded in the physical field. Gauge transformations are not Lorentz\nchanges of inertial frame; the similar names are easy to confuse. In\nnontrivial topology a single smooth global potential may not exist even\nif local expressions are valid. Any concrete calculation also requires\nexplicit initial and boundary conditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Gauge choices change potential descriptions, not the fields. In\nsource-free space the same Maxwell equations support electromagnetic\nwaves.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Electromagnetic Waves in\nVacuum<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/electromagnetism/\">David Tong – Electromagnetism<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Individual Chapters 1–6, lines 4, 14–17; Subject relevance to lesson: M10-L2 – Time-Dependent Potentials and Gauge Freedom · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-033-introduction-to-relativity-and-spacetime-physics-fall-2024/resources/lecture-notes/\">8.033 Introduction to Relativity and Spacetime Physics – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · LEC 02, 04–09, 11–12; Subject relevance to lesson: M10-L2 – Time-Dependent Potentials and Gauge Freedom · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M10-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M10-L3 – Electromagnetic Waves in Vacuum<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor: Advanced Electrodynamics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Faraday’s law, Ampère–Maxwell law and\nvector identities. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive the wave equation from\nMaxwell and relate polarization, frequency and wavelength.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Fields with No Local Sources<\/h2>\n<p>An electromagnetic wave requires no mechanical medium. In a\nsource-free vacuum region, electric and magnetic field changes are\ncoupled in space and time through Maxwell’s equations. For a plane\nfree-space wave, both fields are transverse to the propagation\ndirection. The observation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>⟂<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol E\\perp\\boldsymbol B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoes not imply two independent oscillators: it describes a single\nelectromagnetic phenomenon. Plane waves are idealized, however. A finite\nlaser beam or pulse has a limited spatial extent and can be described by\nsuperposing appropriate modes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Deriving the Wave Equation<\/h2>\n<p>In source-free vacuum,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol E=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol B=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla\\times\\boldsymbol E=-\\partial_t\\boldsymbol B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla\\times\\boldsymbol B=\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0\\partial_t\\boldsymbol E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTaking the curl of Faraday’s equation gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msubsup><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla\\times(\\nabla\\times\\boldsymbol E)=-\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0\\partial_t^2\\boldsymbol E.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Using\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>∇<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla\\times(\\nabla\\times\\boldsymbol E)=\\nabla(\\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol E)-\\nabla^2\\boldsymbol E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>∇<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><msubsup><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla^2\\boldsymbol E-\\frac{1}{c^2}\\partial_t^2\\boldsymbol E=0,\\qquad c=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu_0\\varepsilon_0}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The magnetic field\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsatisfies the same wave equation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Field Amplitude and Wavelength<\/h2>\n<p>A linearly polarized wave traveling in the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndirection may be written as<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒆<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>c<\/mi><\/mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒆<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol E=E_0\\cos(kx-\\omega t)\\boldsymbol e_y,\\quad\\boldsymbol B=\\frac{E_0}{c}\\cos(kx-\\omega t)\\boldsymbol e_z.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=ck<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=c/f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=100\\,\\mathrm{MHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.00<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mn>8<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c\\approx3.00\\times10^8\\,\\mathrm{m/s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe wavelength is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda\\approx3.00\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_0=300\\,\\mathrm{V/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe magnetic amplitude is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_0\\approx1.00\\,\\mathrm{\\mu T}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure places the two field directions at right angles at a common\nphase; propagation is along neither\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_218_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Electric and magnetic oscillations transverse to a common propagation axis\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Electric and magnetic oscillations\ntransverse to a common propagation axis<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>E, B and propagation directions are mutually\nperpendicular.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which Maxwell equation contains the term with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_t\\boldsymbol E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=50\\,\\mathrm{MHz}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>600<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_0=600\\,\\mathrm{V/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The Ampère–Maxwell law.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>6.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=c/f\\approx6.0\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_0=E_0/c\\approx2.0\\,\\mathrm{\\mu T}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transversality and the Approximation<\/h2>\n<p>Transversality also follows from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol k\\cdot\\boldsymbol E_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol k\\cdot\\boldsymbol B_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFaraday’s law relates their amplitudes through\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol B_0=\\boldsymbol k\\times\\boldsymbol E_0/\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAny linear superposition of appropriate solutions satisfies the\nsource-free linear equations. In matter, dispersion, absorption and\nlongitudinal components can appear; a finite beam is not completely\ndescribed by a single pair of infinite sinusoidal curves. The vacuum\nlight speed is the propagation speed of free plane waves, not\nautomatically the energy or group velocity in every medium.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Maxwell equations permit wave propagation and fix the field\namplitudes. The fields also transport energy and momentum.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Energy, Poynting Vector and\nField Momentum<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/electromagnetism/\">David Tong – Electromagnetism<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Individual Chapters 1–6, lines 4, 14–17; Subject relevance to lesson: M10-L3 – Electromagnetic Waves in Vacuum · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-033-introduction-to-relativity-and-spacetime-physics-fall-2024/resources/lecture-notes/\">8.033 Introduction to Relativity and Spacetime Physics – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · LEC 02, 04–09, 11–12; Subject relevance to lesson: M10-L3 – Electromagnetic Waves in Vacuum · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M10-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M10-L4 – Energy, Poynting Vector and Field Momentum<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor: Advanced Electrodynamics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Vacuum Maxwell equations, dot and\ncross products. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive field-energy\nconservation, calculate plane-wave intensity and explain field\nmomentum.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Energy in Electric and Magnetic Fields<\/h2>\n<p>Field energy is measurable: an antenna receives power, a photovoltaic\ncell is illuminated, and light can exert a force. In classical field\ntheory, energy is distributed spatially. Its local change is balanced by\nwork on charged particles and energy transport across the boundary of a\nchosen volume. This local balance distinguishes sources, storage and\ntransfer. For a traveling plane wave, the Poynting vector points along\npropagation; in near fields or standing waves its local time-dependent\npattern can be more complicated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Local Balance and Poynting Theorem<\/h2>\n<p>Electromagnetic energy density in vacuum is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>E<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=(\\varepsilon_0E^2+B^2/\\mu_0)/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTaking appropriate dot products of Ampère–Maxwell and Faraday equations\nand using\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla\\cdot(\\boldsymbol E\\times\\boldsymbol B)=\\boldsymbol B\\cdot(\\nabla\\times\\boldsymbol E)-\\boldsymbol E\\cdot(\\nabla\\times\\boldsymbol B)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>+<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑺<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑱<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑺<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t}+\\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol S=-\\boldsymbol J\\cdot\\boldsymbol E,\\qquad\\boldsymbol S=\\frac{1}{\\mu_0}\\boldsymbol E\\times\\boldsymbol B.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The SI units of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑺<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nVolume integration and the divergence theorem turn the spatial term into\nenergy crossing the boundary. The term\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑱<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol J\\cdot\\boldsymbol E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the power delivered by the field to matter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Plane-Wave Intensity<\/h2>\n<p>For a monochromatic plane vacuum wave,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=E/c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>E<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=\\varepsilon_0E^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nInstantaneous energy flux is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>c<\/mi><msup><mi>E<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S=\\varepsilon_0cE^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nits time average is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>c<\/mi><msubsup><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=\\langle S\\rangle=\\tfrac12\\varepsilon_0cE_0^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With peak electric amplitude\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_0=100\\,\\mathrm{V/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>c<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.654<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_0c\\approx2.654\\times10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{A/V}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe average intensity is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>13.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I\\approx13.3\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram connects flux, an illuminated surface and transferred power.\nThis value uses the <em>peak<\/em> amplitude of a sinusoid, not its rms\namplitude.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_224_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Electromagnetic wave carrying energy toward a marked target surface\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Electromagnetic wave carrying energy\ntoward a marked target surface<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Poynting vector shows the plane wave’s energy flow.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does the minus sign before\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑱<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol J\\cdot\\boldsymbol E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmean?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non an absorbing target at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=300\\,\\mathrm{W/m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Positive work on current removes energy from the electromagnetic\nfield.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincreases by a factor of four, because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><msubsup><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I\\propto E_0^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.00<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=I/c\\approx1.00\\times10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm{Pa}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Momentum Flux and Physical Limits<\/h2>\n<p>The field carries momentum density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒈<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑺<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol g=\\boldsymbol S/c^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin vacuum. For normally incident radiation fully absorbed by a surface,\nmean pressure is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=I/c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nan ideal mirror doubles normal momentum transfer to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>I<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=2I/c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Maxwell stress tensor describes spatial fluxes of field momentum and\nsupports force calculations on surfaces. In matter the allocation of\nfield and mechanical momentum must be treated consistently; dispersive\nand absorbing media require more than a naive use of vacuum formulas. A\npositive incoming intensity does not establish that all energy is\nabsorbed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The Poynting vector balances energy and field momentum gives\nradiation pressure. Time-varying sources can radiate such fields.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Retarded Potentials and Dipole\nRadiation<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/electromagnetism/\">David Tong – Electromagnetism<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Individual Chapters 1–6, lines 4, 14–17; Subject relevance to lesson: M10-L4 – Energy, Poynting Vector and Field Momentum · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-033-introduction-to-relativity-and-spacetime-physics-fall-2024/resources/lecture-notes/\">8.033 Introduction to Relativity and Spacetime Physics – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · LEC 02, 04–09, 11–12; Subject relevance to lesson: M10-L4 – Energy, Poynting Vector and Field Momentum · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M10-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M10-L5 – Retarded Potentials and Dipole Radiation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor: Advanced Electrodynamics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Potentials, wave equation, harmonic\nmotion and Poynting vector. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain retarded source\nrelations and the far field of an electric dipole in the nonrelativistic\nlimit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why Radiation Has a Travel Time<\/h2>\n<p>Changes in a remote charge distribution are not observed instantly.\nThe wave equations for electromagnetic potentials have causal retarded\nsolutions: fields measured at time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndepend on earlier source configurations. A static electric dipole\npossesses an electrostatic field but does not radiate power into the far\nzone within the ideal model. Time dependence, particularly the second\nderivative of dipole moment, creates transverse radiation in the\nnonrelativistic dipole approximation. Near and far fields must be\ndistinguished because they exhibit different distance dependences.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Retarded Sources and Far Fields<\/h2>\n<p>For observation point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand source point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol r&#39;<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nretarded time is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_r=t-|\\boldsymbol r-\\boldsymbol r&#39;|/c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn Lorenz gauge, the scalar potential in vacuum is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>∫<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi(\\boldsymbol r,t)=\\frac{1}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0}\\int\\frac{\\rho(\\boldsymbol r&#39;,t_r)}{|\\boldsymbol r-\\boldsymbol r&#39;|}\\,\\mathrm d^3r&#39;.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A corresponding formula holds for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑱<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_0\\boldsymbol J/(4\\pi)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a compact source in nonrelativistic motion with electric dipole\nmoment\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒑<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol p(t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe radiation-zone field is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≃<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒏<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒑<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>×<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒏<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒏<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><mi>c<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol E_{\\mathrm{rad}}(\\boldsymbol r,t)\\simeq\\frac{\\boldsymbol n\\times(\\boldsymbol p&#39;&#39;(t-r/c)\\times\\boldsymbol n)}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0c^2r},\\qquad\\boldsymbol B_{\\mathrm{rad}}=\\frac{\\boldsymbol n\\times\\boldsymbol E_{\\mathrm{rad}}}{c}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒏<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol n=\\boldsymbol r/r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Radiation Angle and Frequency<\/h2>\n<p>Let a dipole along the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\naxis oscillate as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒑<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒆<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol p(t)=p_0\\cos(\\omega t)\\boldsymbol e_z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒑<\/mi><mo>″<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\boldsymbol p&#39;&#39;|=\\omega^2p_0|\\cos(\\omega t)|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFar-field amplitude varies with angle\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom the dipole axis as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso mean radiated intensity varies as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>θ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin^2\\theta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAlong the dipole axis\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nideal dipole radiation vanishes; it is maximal in the equatorial plane\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=\\pi/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nDoubling angular frequency while holding\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfixed increases mean power by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^4=16<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin this dipole model. The graphic gives a two-dimensional slice through\nthe toroidal angular pattern rather than an isotropic spherical\nwave.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_247_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two-dimensional polar plot showing side lobes and dipole axis\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two-dimensional polar plot showing side\nlobes and dipole axis<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Maximum transverse to the dipole axis, zero along the\naxis.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Define retarded time for separation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">In which direction is ideal dipole radiation maximal?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does dipole radiation power change under\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>↦<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>ω<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega\\mapsto3\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t_r=t-R/c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Perpendicular to the dipole axis, at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=\\pi/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">By a factor of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>3<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>81<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3^4=81<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of the Dipole Model<\/h2>\n<p>The dipole approximation requires source size much smaller than the\nwavelength and nonrelativistic motion. Extended sources demand phase\ndifferences, higher multipoles and antenna geometry. Retarded-potential\nsolutions must also be consistent with charge continuity,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑱<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_t\\rho+\\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol J=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRadiation fields decay as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand mean intensity as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>r<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/r^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhereas near-field terms can fall faster. Finite propagation time does\nnot imply isotropic radiation, nor does a purely classical point-dipole\nmodel describe individual emitted photons; those require\nquantization.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Retarded fields connect sources to observations causally. Entering\nmatter introduces polarization, dispersion and interface conditions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Electromagnetic Fields in\nMatter<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/electromagnetism/\">David Tong – Electromagnetism<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Individual Chapters 1–6, lines 4, 14–17; Subject relevance to lesson: M10-L5 – Retarded Potentials and Dipole Radiation · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M10-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M10-L6 – Electromagnetic Fields in Matter<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor: Advanced Electrodynamics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Maxwell equations, complex numbers,\nplane waves and elementary optics. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain constitutive laws,\nboundary conditions, refraction and absorption in simple linear\nmedia.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Microscopic Response and Macroscopic Field<\/h2>\n<p>Matter contains bound and free charges. Many applications average\nmicroscopic fields over small volumes and introduce polarization\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑷<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand magnetization\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑴<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe macroscopic auxiliary fields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑫<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑯<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nseparate free from bound sources and enable models with measured\nparameters. Refractive index is not a universal constant of a substance:\nit may depend on frequency, temperature, propagation direction and\npolarization. The simple isotropic linear model is a starting point, not\na universal law of matter.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Constitutive Laws and Interfaces<\/h2>\n<p>In SI units define\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑫<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑷<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol D=\\varepsilon_0\\boldsymbol E+\\boldsymbol P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑯<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑴<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol H=\\boldsymbol B/\\mu_0-\\boldsymbol M<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a homogeneous isotropic linear nondispersive model, take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑫<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol D=\\varepsilon\\boldsymbol E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑯<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol B=\\mu\\boldsymbol H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIntegrated Maxwell equations imply interface jump conditions with unit\nnormal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒏<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒏<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑫<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑫<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒏<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol n\\cdot(\\boldsymbol D_2-\\boldsymbol D_1)=\\sigma_f,\\quad\\boldsymbol n\\cdot(\\boldsymbol B_2-\\boldsymbol B_1)=0,<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒏<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒏<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑯<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑯<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑲<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol n\\times(\\boldsymbol E_2-\\boldsymbol E_1)=0,\\quad\\boldsymbol n\\times(\\boldsymbol H_2-\\boldsymbol H_1)=\\boldsymbol K_f.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The symbols\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑲<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol K_f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenote free surface charge and surface current density.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Normal Incidence<\/h2>\n<p>For two nonmagnetic lossless media at normal incidence, the\nelectric-field reflection amplitude is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_E=\\frac{n_1-n_2}{n_1+n_2},\\qquad R=|r_E|^2.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At an air\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_1\\approx1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nto glass\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_2\\approx1.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\ninterface,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_E=-0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.04<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=0.04<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\napproximately four percent of incident power is reflected at one ideal\nboundary. The negative sign signifies phase inversion of the reflected\nelectric field. A finite window with two boundaries also exhibits\nmultiple reflections. The figure separates incident, reflected and\ntransmitted directions and illustrates their energy accounting.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_164_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Wave arrows at an air–glass boundary showing reflected and transmitted intensity fractions\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Wave arrows at an air–glass boundary\nshowing reflected and transmitted intensity fractions<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Normal incidence: incident, reflected and transmitted\nwaves.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which free source determines the jump in normal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_1=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_2=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat normal incidence.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R+T=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot hold in every material?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Free surface charge density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>9<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>11.1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=((1-2)/(1+2))^2=1/9\\approx11.1\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Absorption or other loss channels can remove power from the\npropagating field.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Dispersion, Conductivity and Model Limits<\/h2>\n<p>The expression\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_r\\mu_r}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napplies directly only to suitably defined homogeneous isotropic models\nand requires complex frequency-dependent material parameters when\ndispersion is present. In absorbing media,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis complex and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R+T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be less than one; the missing energy is absorbed. Anisotropic\ncrystals may be birefringent, while conducting media can exhibit\npenetration depth instead of ordinary transparency. At oblique incidence\nFresnel coefficients depend on polarization. Ray diagrams thus represent\na special limit of electromagnetic wave theory and do not replace\nMaxwell boundary conditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Material laws govern macroscopic propagation. Next the same\nelectromagnetic fields are examined from different inertial frames.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Relativistic Transformation of\nElectric and Magnetic Fields<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/electromagnetism/\">David Tong – Electromagnetism<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Individual Chapters 1–6, lines 4, 14–17; Subject relevance to lesson: M10-L6 – Electromagnetic Fields in Matter · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-033-introduction-to-relativity-and-spacetime-physics-fall-2024/resources/lecture-notes/\">8.033 Introduction to Relativity and Spacetime Physics – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · LEC 02, 04–09, 11–12; Subject relevance to lesson: M10-L6 – Electromagnetic Fields in Matter · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M10-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M10-L7 – Relativistic Transformation of Electric and Magnetic Fields<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor: Advanced Electrodynamics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Special relativity, Lorentz factor\nand Maxwell equations. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Apply field transformations\nunder a Lorentz boost and identify common invariants.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Electromagnetism in Moving Frames<\/h2>\n<p>Electric and magnetic fields are not two independently transforming\nobjects. Their division depends on the observer’s state of motion. A\nfield purely electric in one inertial frame can acquire a magnetic\ncomponent in a moving frame. Physical predictions for a charged test\nparticle nevertheless remain consistent. The change is a Lorentz\ntransformation rather than Galilean velocity addition. Signs depend on\nhow the relative boost is defined, so its direction must be stated\nexplicitly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Parallel and Transverse Field Components<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>S<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S&#39;<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmove at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒆<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol v=v\\boldsymbol e_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrelative to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=(1-v^2/c^2)^{-1/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe components parallel to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare unchanged:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>∥<\/mo><mo>′<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>∥<\/mo><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol E&#39;_{\\parallel}=\\boldsymbol E_{\\parallel}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>∥<\/mo><mo>′<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>∥<\/mo><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol B&#39;_{\\parallel}=\\boldsymbol B_{\\parallel}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor perpendicular components,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>⟂<\/mo><mo>′<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>⟂<\/mo><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>⟂<\/mo><mo>′<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>⟂<\/mo><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><mo>×<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><\/mrow><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol E&#39;_{\\perp}=\\gamma(\\boldsymbol E_{\\perp}+\\boldsymbol v\\times\\boldsymbol B),\\quad\\boldsymbol B&#39;_{\\perp}=\\gamma\\left(\\boldsymbol B_{\\perp}-\\frac{\\boldsymbol v\\times\\boldsymbol E}{c^2}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>These are passive transformations to measurements in the moving\nframe. Replacing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><mo>↦<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒗<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol v\\mapsto-\\boldsymbol v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the inverse transformation. Longitudinal field components do not\nmix.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Magnetic Field from an Electric Field<\/h2>\n<p>In\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ntake a uniform purely electric field\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒆<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol E=E_0\\boldsymbol e_y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol B=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn observer in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>S<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S&#39;<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmoving in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeasures\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E&#39;_y=\\gamma E_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mfrac><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B&#39;_z=-\\gamma\\frac{vE_0}{c^2}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=0.6c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=1.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1000<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_0=1000\\,\\mathrm{V/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\none finds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>E<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1250<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E&#39;_y=1250\\,\\mathrm{V/m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>B<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B&#39;_z\\approx-2.50\\,\\mathrm{\\mu T}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe negative sign indicates the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndirection. The diagram shows a boost along\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe original\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfield and the induced measured magnetic component along\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nit is not a new physical source.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_158_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Comparison of electric and magnetic field directions before and after a Lorentz boost\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Comparison of electric and magnetic field\ndirections before and after a Lorentz boost<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Motion along x mixes transverse field components.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which field components stay unchanged under a boost along\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=0.8c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which two combinations are Lorentz invariant?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mn>0.8<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.667<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=1/\\sqrt{1-0.8^2}=5/3\\approx1.667<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>E<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B^2-E^2/c^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol E\\cdot\\boldsymbol B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Field Invariants and Interpretation Limits<\/h2>\n<p>Two combinations remain unchanged under every Lorentz boost:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><msup><mi>E<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_1=B^2-\\frac{E^2}{c^2},\\qquad I_2=\\boldsymbol E\\cdot\\boldsymbol B.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In the example\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_2=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin both frames, while\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><msup><mi><\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>E<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><msup><mi><\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msubsup><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B&#39;^2-E&#39;^2/c^2=-E_0^2/c^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese invariants help determine whether a frame exists in which one\nfield vanishes. A special field configuration does not justify general\nclaims about the underlying sources. Accelerated observers require\nadditional care in coordinate and measurement definitions. The\ntransformation laws motivate a compact tensor formulation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Field transformations mix electric and magnetic quantities. An\nantisymmetric field tensor unifies them in one mathematical\nstructure.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Electromagnetic Field Tensor and\nCovariant Maxwell Equations<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/electromagnetism/\">David Tong – Electromagnetism<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Individual Chapters 1–6, lines 4, 14–17; Subject relevance to lesson: M10-L7 – Relativistic Transformation of Electric and Magnetic Fields · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-033-introduction-to-relativity-and-spacetime-physics-fall-2024/resources/lecture-notes/\">8.033 Introduction to Relativity and Spacetime Physics – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · LEC 02, 04–09, 11–12; Subject relevance to lesson: M10-L7 – Relativistic Transformation of Electric and Magnetic Fields · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M10-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M10-L8 – Electromagnetic Field Tensor and Covariant Maxwell Equations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M10 – Bachelor: Advanced Electrodynamics<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Four-vectors, index notation, Lorentz\ntransformations and Maxwell equations. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Define the field tensor with a\nfixed metric, compute two invariants and write Maxwell’s equations\ncovariantly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Why an Antisymmetric Tensor?<\/h2>\n<p>Electric and magnetic components mix when the inertial frame changes.\nA tensor with two spacetime indices encodes that mixing. Antisymmetry\nreduces sixteen matrix entries to six independent components: three\nelectric and three magnetic. The tensor lets Maxwell equations retain\ntheir form under Lorentz transformations. Such calculations require an\nexplicit metric and component definitions. Changing conventions midway\nthrough a derivation produces apparent sign errors; copying a matrix\nfrom a differently defined source does not resolve them.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Definitions and Covariant Form<\/h2>\n<p>Choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^\\mu=(ct,x,y,z)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand metric\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta_{\\mu\\nu}=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,+1,+1,+1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDefine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑨<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A^\\mu=(\\phi/c,\\boldsymbol A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>η<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial^\\mu=\\eta^{\\mu\\nu}\\partial_\\nu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msup><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/msup><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F^{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial^\\mu A^\\nu-\\partial^\\nu A^\\mu.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>It follows that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F^{0i}=E_i/c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>ε<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F^{ij}=\\varepsilon^{ijk}B_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe matrix is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F^{\\mu\\nu}=\\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;E_x/c&amp;E_y/c&amp;E_z/c\\\\-E_x/c&amp;0&amp;B_z&amp;-B_y\\\\-E_y/c&amp;-B_z&amp;0&amp;B_x\\\\-E_z/c&amp;B_y&amp;-B_x&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>J<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑱<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J^\\mu=(c\\rho,\\boldsymbol J)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe inhomogeneous Maxwell equations become<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>J<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_\\mu F^{\\mu\\nu}=-\\mu_0J^\\nu.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The homogeneous equations can be expressed as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_\\lambda F_{\\mu\\nu}+\\partial_\\mu F_{\\nu\\lambda}+\\partial_\\nu F_{\\lambda\\mu}=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Example: Full Tensor Contraction<\/h2>\n<p>Consider\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒆<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol E=E_0\\boldsymbol e_x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑩<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒆<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol B=B_0\\boldsymbol e_z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe only independent nonzero entries are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mn>01<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F^{01}=E_0/c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F^{12}=B_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nplus their antisymmetric counterparts. Lowering indices with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>η<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchanges the sign only for time-space components:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mn>01<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{01}=-E_0/c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{12}=B_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe <em>full<\/em> Einstein sum therefore gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msubsup><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F^{\\mu\\nu}F_{\\mu\\nu}=2\\left(B_0^2-\\frac{E_0^2}{c^2}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe answer is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><msubsup><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2E_0^2/c^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msubsup><mi>B<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2B_0^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram shows the six independent matrix positions and their\nantisymmetric partners; each pair appears twice in the complete\ncontraction.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_097_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Four by four electromagnetic field tensor matrix showing electric and magnetic entries\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four by four electromagnetic field tensor\nmatrix showing electric and magnetic entries<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Six independent components, paired with opposite sign.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many independent entries does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F^{\\mu\\nu}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhave in four dimensions?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F^{\\mu\\nu}F_{\\mu\\nu}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\boldsymbol E=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which elements must be checked together when changing sign\nconventions?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Six:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\cdot3/2=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>B<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2B^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Definition of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nits matrix, potentials, metric, and the sign of the inhomogeneous\nMaxwell equation.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sign Conventions and Verification<\/h2>\n<p>The displayed field-tensor matrix depends on metric signature,\npotential convention and the definition of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F^{\\mu\\nu}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith the present choices,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∇<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑬<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_iF^{i0}=-\\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol E/c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe inhomogeneous Maxwell equation therefore needs a negative sign:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>J<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_\\mu F^{\\mu\\nu}=-\\mu_0J^\\nu.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>That sign follows from the explicit definition of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F^{\\mu\\nu}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the metric, rather than being an independent extra convention. The\ntime component\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nreproduces Gauss’s law and spatial components\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu=i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\ngive Ampère–Maxwell. Using the alternative definition\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>↦<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F\\mapsto-F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchanges both signs together and is equally valid.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and Outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The tensor unifies electrodynamics with special relativity.\nConsistency must be checked against electric Gauss’s law and the full\ntensor contraction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/electromagnetism/\">David Tong – Electromagnetism<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Individual Chapters 1–6, lines 4, 14–17; Subject relevance to lesson: M10-L8 – Electromagnetic Field Tensor and Covariant Maxwell Equations · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-033-introduction-to-relativity-and-spacetime-physics-fall-2024/resources/lecture-notes/\">8.033 Introduction to Relativity and Spacetime Physics – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · LEC 02, 04–09, 11–12; Subject relevance to lesson: M10-L8 – Electromagnetic Field Tensor and Covariant Maxwell Equations · Last accessed: 2026-11-20<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://galileoandeinstein.phys.virginia.edu/Elec_Mag/2022_Lectures/EM_65_Rel_Electrodynamics.html\">University of Virginia – Relativistic Electrodynamics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · lines 48–73, 106–119; Subject relevance to lesson: M10-L8 – Electromagnetic Field Tensor and Covariant Maxwell Equations<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
